>
  <h2 id="one-thread">One thread, one task</h2>
  <p>
    A <Term def="One conversation with one agent about one task. You can start as many as you like, and each one knows only what was said in it.">thread</Term>
    is one conversation with one agent about one task. You ask the agent to fix the holiday hours bug on the bakery’s
    website; it reads the code, makes a change, runs a check and tells you what it did. Everything it knows about the job is
    in that conversation, and nothing from your other conversations leaks in.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep a thread to one task. When the bug is fixed, the thread is finished. Start a fresh one for the next job, even in
    the same project, so the agent is not carrying the old task around in its head. The <a href={url('explain/context/')}>context</a> page explains why a long, mixed conversation makes an agent worse.
  </p>

  <h2 id="several-threads">Several threads, and you in the middle</h2>
  <p>
    The simplest way to run more than one agent is to open more than one thread. While one agent fixes the holiday hours,
    another can tidy the exported contacts, and a third can add a payments column to the club list. They do not know about
    each other. You are the coordinator: you start each one, check each result and decide what happens next.
  </p>
  <p>
    This works well while the tasks do not touch each other and you can keep up with the reviewing. The second condition
    is the one that bites. Agents finish faster than a person can check their work, and an unchecked result is not done.
    Two threads is a comfortable start; add a third when two leave you waiting.
  </p>
  <Deeper level="technical" title="Threads in practice">
    <p>
      In a terminal, a thread is a session: a second terminal tab running <code>claude</code> or <code>codex</code> in the same
      project is a second thread. In T3 Code a thread is an item in the sidebar, and you can start one, send it off and go back to
      another without waiting. Threads in the same folder share files on disk, which is the reason for worktrees further down.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <h2 id="subagents">Helpers your agent starts itself</h2>
  <p>
    A <Term def="A helper agent that your agent starts on its own to do one job. It begins with an empty memory, does the job, and hands back a short report.">subagent</Term>
    is a helper that your agent starts by itself, inside the thread you are already in. It gets its own fresh memory, does
    one job, and hands back a short summary. You did not open it and you usually do not talk to it.
  </p>
  <p>
    That matters for two reasons. The first is the main agent’s memory. Searching a large codebase or running a long test
    suite produces pages of output, and if the main agent read all of it, that output would crowd out the task. A helper
    reads the pages and returns a paragraph, so the main conversation stays clean. The second is time: several helpers can
    work side by side, so a search across the club list’s pages, its database code and its tests can happen at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cost of that clean memory is that the main agent only sees the summary. If the helper missed something, or
    summarised it away, the main agent does not know. A summary is the helper’s account of what it found, and it can be
    wrong in a confident voice.
  </p>
  <Deeper level="technical" title="How to define a subagent and how to ask for one">
    <p>
      <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.subagentFiles" show="claim" />
      <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.subagentModel" show="claim" /> A test runner for the bakery site
      could look like this:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{subagentFile}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The description is what the main agent reads when it decides whether to use the helper, so write it as a trigger: what the
      helper does and when to call it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Fact id="harnesses.codex.subagentFiles" show="claim" /> <Fact id="harnesses.codex.subagentModel" show="claim" />
    </p>
    <pre><code>{codexAgent}</code></pre>
    <p>
      You do not need a file to get a subagent. Ask in plain words: “use a subagent to find every place the opening hours are
      computed, and report the file names”, or in Codex “spawn two agents, one per page”. Say what you want back and whether the
      main agent should wait for it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Helpers run in the foreground or the background. A foreground helper blocks the conversation until it reports; a background
      helper runs while the main agent carries on. <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.subagentBackground" show="claim" />
      <Fact id="harnesses.codex.subagentResults" show="claim" />
    </p>
  </Deeper>
  <Deeper level="engineer" title="Context isolation and what a hand-back loses">
    <p>
      <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.subagentFreshContext" show="claim" /> The isolation is the point and the hazard. The helper
      does not know the constraints you agreed with the main agent ten turns ago unless the spawn prompt repeats them, and the
      main agent receives a few hundred tokens distilled from tens of thousands the helper read. Everything between the two is a
      lossy channel written by a model.
    </p>
    <p>
      Treat the spawn prompt as the helper’s entire world: the goal, the files, the constraints, what done looks like and the
      shape of the report. Ask for evidence in the report (file paths and line numbers, the failing test’s name, the command and
      its exit code) so the main agent can check a claim instead of trusting it. Ask for what the helper did not look at, too.
    </p>
    <p>
      Parallel helpers that write to the same files race. Keep writing helpers on disjoint files, or give each one its own
      worktree (below), or make helpers read-only and let the main agent do the edits.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <h2 id="nesting">Helpers that start helpers</h2>
  <p>
    A helper can start helpers of its own. In Claude Code the default limit is <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.nestingDepth" /> layers
    below your conversation. Codex limits how many helpers can run at once but does not publish a depth limit.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each layer multiplies. One lead with three helpers, each with three of their own, is thirteen agents, and each one
    reads files and writes text against your allowance. Each layer also loses detail: the bottom helper’s finding reaches you
    as a summary of a summary. Use nesting for big jobs that split cleanly into parts, and expect to check more at the end.
  </p>
  <Deeper level="engineer" title="Depth limits, environment variables and agent teams">
    <p>
      <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.nestingDepth" show="claim" /> Setting it to 1 turns nesting off, which is a reasonable default
      for reviewers: a reviewer that starts its own helpers turns one review into several. On the Codex side,
      <Fact id="harnesses.codex.maxThreads" show="claim" /> The Codex docs do not state a nesting depth.
    </p>
    <p>
      Claude Code also has agent teams, a separate feature where several full Claude Code instances work together and message each
      other. <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.agentTeams" show="claim" /> Anthropic’s own figure for their cost is about
      <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.agentTeamTokens" /> the tokens of a standard session when teammates run in plan mode.
      <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.agentTeamSize" show="claim" />
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <h2 id="model-sizes">Big models and small ones together</h2>
  <p>
    Labs sell models in sizes. The largest are the most capable and the slowest and dearest per word; the smallest are fast and
    cheap and make more mistakes. A team can mix them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The common arrangement is a top model in charge and small models doing the volume work: searching files, sorting a pile of
    contacts into duplicates and non-duplicates, running checks and reporting. That suits jobs where most attempts are cheap to
    check and many attempts beat one careful one. The reverse also works: a cheaper model does the routine work and calls a top
    model only for a decision it cannot make or for a review at the end. That suits long, mostly routine jobs with a few hard
    moments.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trap is a small model on work it cannot do. A cheap model that fails and retries five times costs more than a capable one
    that gets it right once, and its failures take your time to find.
  </p>
  <Estimate name={fanout.person} href={`${fanout.url}&t=${22 * 60 + 23}s`} date={fanout.published}>
    <p><Fact id="observations.estimates.theo-2026-10-09-haiku-fanout.fact" show="claim" /></p>
  </Estimate>
  <Opinion date={opinions.decisions.verified}>
    <p>{opinions.decisions.value}</p>
  </Opinion>
  <Deeper level="technical" title="Setting a helper’s model">
    <p>
      In Claude Code, set <code>model:</code> in the subagent file, or name a model when you ask: “use a haiku subagent to list every
      file that mentions opening hours”. <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.smallSubagentModel" show="claim" /> In Codex, set
      <code>agents.default_subagent_model</code> in <code>config.toml</code> or <code>model</code> in the agent’s own file.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <h2 id="across-providers">Handing work to another lab’s model</h2>
  <p>
    Models from one lab tend to share blind spots, because they are trained in similar ways. A model from another lab reviewing
    the work is closer to a second person reading it. A common pattern is one lab’s model writing the fix for the holiday hours
    and another lab’s model reviewing the diff.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can do this by hand: copy the change into a thread with another provider and ask for a review. T3 Code does it inside one
    thread. You can switch the provider mid-conversation, and T3 Code passes a selection of the conversation to the new model. It
    does not ask the old model to write a summary. What carries over is the conversation itself, trimmed to fit; the new model does
    not inherit the old one’s private reasoning or its tool state.
  </p>
  <Deeper level="engineer" title="Cross-provider handoff as a budgeted selection">
    <p>
      <Fact id="t3code.handoff" show="claim" /> <Fact id="t3code.handoffLeavesOut" show="claim" />
      <Fact id="t3code.handoffCap" show="claim" />
    </p>
    <p>
      The consequence is that a handoff is lossy by construction, but the losses are mechanical: older turns drop out, reasoning
      and attachments do not travel. Before a handoff for review, put the contract in the latest message: the goal, the diff or the
      branch to read, the checks to run, and what a pass means. Then the receiving model needs nothing from the trimmed history.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <h2 id="worktrees">Separate copies of the code</h2>
  <p>
    Two agents working in the same folder are two people typing in the same document. One saves a file while the other is halfway
    through changing it, and both end up confused. A
    <Term def="A second working copy of the same Git repository, on its own branch, in its own folder. Changes in one copy do not appear in the other until you merge them.">worktree</Term>
    gives each agent its own copy of the code on its own branch, so parallel work stays apart until you merge it. If you run more
    than one agent on one project at the same time, give each its own worktree.
  </p>
  <Deeper level="technical" title="Worktrees in each tool">
    <p>
      <Fact id="t3code.worktrees" show="claim" /> In Claude Code, <code>claude --worktree</code> starts a session in a new worktree,
      and a subagent can get its own: <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.subagentWorktree" show="claim" />
    </p>
    <p>
      A worktree isolates files, not everything else. Two worktrees still share the database on your machine, the ports a dev
      server listens on and any external service the code talks to. Give each its own port and, for the club list, its own copy of
      the test database.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <h2 id="cost">What a team costs on a $20 plan</h2>
  <p>
    Assume a plan at <Fact id="plans.claude.pro.price" /> a month, the price of both Claude Pro and ChatGPT Plus
    (<Fact id="plans.chatgpt.plus.price" />). A plan gives you an allowance that refills every five hours and is also capped per
    week. Every agent you run draws from that one allowance. <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.subagentUsage" show="claim" />
  </p>
  <p>
    A team spends that allowance faster than one conversation does. A thread with four helpers can use in an hour
    what a single thread uses in an afternoon, and on a $20 plan a busy team can empty a five-hour window well before the five
    hours are up. Then you wait. Switching to another model does not help: <Fact id="plans.claude.limits.sharedAcrossModels" show="claim" />
    The <a href={url('explain/plans-and-api/')}>plans page</a> explains the windows and the weekly cap.
  </p>
  <p>
    A sane default: work in one thread until you trust how the agent works. Then run two threads on unrelated tasks. Let the agent
    use helpers when it wants to search or test, which it will do on its own. Reach for a lead with helpers, a council or nesting
    only for a job that clearly needs one, and watch the usage meter the first time.
  </p>
  <CopyPrompt title="Ask for helpers without losing control" id="prompt-helpers">{`Add a "paid this season" column to the club list's members page.

Before you change anything, use up to two read-only subagents: one to find where the members list is built, one to find how payments are stored. Each should report file paths and line numbers, not a summary of the whole codebase. Do not let a subagent start subagents of its own.

Then make the change yourself, run the tests and show me the diff. If the subagents disagree about where payments live, stop and ask me.`}</CopyPrompt>
  <Deeper level="engineer" title="Usage accounting">
    <p>
      <Fact id="plans.claude.limits.whatUsageMeans" show="claim" /> <Fact id="plans.chatgpt.limits.estimates" show="claim" />
      Neither lab publishes a figure you could divide by an agent count, so the honest planning unit is your own meter after a
      representative hour.
    </p>
    <p>
      <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.subagentAttribution" show="claim" /> That breakdown is the quickest way to see whether helpers are
      worth what they cost on your work. <Fact id="harnesses.codex.subagents" show="claim" />
    </p>
    <p>
      A helper is not free even when it saves the main context. Each spawn pays for its own system prompt, tool definitions and the
      files it reads, and the cache the main thread built does not carry over. A helper that reads three files to answer a question
      the main agent could answer from memory costs more than it saves.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <h2 id="six-ways">Six ways to run a team</h2>
  <p>
    The board runs one job each of these ways, with the same agents and the same person, so the difference you see is the
    structure.
  </p>
  <h3 id="way-solo">One agent</h3>
  <p>
    One thread, one task, and you brief and review each result. It is the baseline for time and for tokens, and the right
    start for almost anything. It fails when the job outgrows one conversation.
  </p>
  <h3 id="way-review">Build, then review</h3>
  <p>
    A builder makes each change and a second agent reviews it before it reaches you. A reviewer from another lab, or at least a
    different model, catches more than a fresh copy of the builder, which shares its blind spots.
    <Fact id="harnesses.claudeCode.codeReview" show="claim" />
  </p>
  <h3 id="way-plan">Plan, build, review</h3>
  <p>
    One agent writes a plan that splits the job, several build the pieces, and one reviews them. You read the plan once instead
    of writing a brief for every piece. Every builder follows the plan faithfully, so a wrong plan is built several times over:
    a cheap planner can waste good builders.
  </p>
  <h3 id="way-parallel">Parallel worktrees</h3>
  <p>
    Several threads, each on its own task in its own worktree, with nobody coordinating them but you. It costs about one solo
    run per thread. It fails when you cannot review as fast as they finish, or when the pieces touch the same code and collide
    when you merge them.
  </p>
  <h3 id="way-lead">Lead and subagents</h3>
  <p>
    One agent hands pieces to subagents, each starting with a fresh context, and gathers their summaries. You talk only to the
    lead, and at the end you review one large combined change. It fails when a summary leaves out the detail that mattered.
  </p>
  <h3 id="way-best">Two models, keep one</h3>
  <p>
    The same task goes to two models and you keep the better result. <Fact id="t3code.manyModels" show="claim" /> It doubles the
    tokens and the reading. It catches the mistakes only one of the two made, and none of those both made.
  </p>
  <p>
    A council, where several models answer and one judges, and nested teams, where helpers start helpers, are variations on
    these. Nesting has its own section above; councils come up below.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-goes-wrong">What goes wrong</h2>
  <p>
    Summaries that sound confident and are wrong. A helper reports that it found every place the hours are computed, and it missed
    one. The main agent builds on the report, and so do you. Ask helpers for evidence you can check, and check the diff yourself
    before you believe the summary.
  </p>
  <p>
    A helper confirming the idea it was handed. If the main agent asks “check that the bug is in the date parsing”, the helper goes
    looking for date-parsing problems and usually finds something. Ask open questions instead: “find out why 25 December shows the
    normal hours”.
  </p>
  <p>
    Runaway loops. An agent told to keep going until every reviewer is satisfied may never stop, because reviewers can always find
    something. Each round adds code, and more code gives the next round more to find.
  </p>
  <Estimate name={runaway.person} href={`${runaway.url}&t=${21 * 60 + 19}s`} date={runaway.published}>
    <p><Fact id="observations.estimates.theo-2026-11-16-council-runaway.fact" show="claim" /></p>
    <p>His fix was a cap on rounds, permission to stop and say the work is going in circles, and an instruction to keep the scope fixed.</p>
  </Estimate>
  <Embed id="TWoS5Nmyoc8" title="Stop Searching For the Perfect Model" start={20 * 60 + 18} />
  <p>
    Put a stop condition in every prompt that starts a team: a number of rounds, a point at which to give up and ask you, and what
    counts as done. See <a href={links.theo.url}>{links.theo.label}</a> for more of his working notes on running councils.
  </p>
  <Deeper level="engineer" title="When a script should orchestrate instead of a model">
    <p>
      A model deciding who runs next is the right tool when the next step depends on judgement. When it does not, a script is
      cheaper and more predictable. If the contact tidy means “split the export into fifty files, clean each, merge the results”,
      write the loop yourself and call the agent once per file in non-interactive mode. The script holds the fan-out, the retry
      policy, the round cap and the merge; the model only does the part that needs a model. You get a fixed number of calls, a
      deterministic order, logs you can rerun from, and no chance of the coordinator wandering off.
    </p>
    <p>
      A rule of thumb: if you can draw the flow before the run starts, put it in code. Leave orchestration to a model when the
      shape of the work is only known once it starts.
    </p>
  </Deeper>

  <h2 id="model">How the model works</h2>
  <p>
    The lab on the board and the smaller pictures around it run a simulation, not a measurement. A job is split into pieces of
    {M.PIECE_MIN} minutes of work each. Every minute, each agent works on a piece, redoes one, checks one, waits for you, or waits
    for a piece another agent has not finished. You handle one thing at a time, first come first served: a brief, a review, a
    merge. Tangled work means more pieces depend on others and more changes collide. Randomness is replaced by fixed sequences,
    so the same settings always give the same run; the count of uncaught mistakes is the expected number, rounded.
  </p>
  <p>The numbers that are not controls are these assumptions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Speed per piece, against a middle-class model: large {M.SPEED.large}×, small {M.SPEED.small}× the time.</li>
    <li>Chance of a mistake in one build: large {M.MISTAKE.large}, middle {M.MISTAKE.middle}, small {M.MISTAKE.small}. A redo takes {M.REDO} of a build and makes mistakes at {M.REDO} of the rate.</li>
    <li>Chance a plan gets one piece’s brief wrong: large {M.PLAN_MISS.large}, middle {M.PLAN_MISS.middle}, small {M.PLAN_MISS.small}.</li>
    <li>Chance an agent reviewer catches a mistake: large {M.CATCH.large}, middle {M.CATCH.middle}, small {M.CATCH.small}; times {M.SAME_MODEL} when it is the same class as the builder; times {M.PLAN_CATCH} for a wrong brief, because it checks against the plan.</li>
    <li>The lead reads summaries, so its catch rate is times {M.SUMMARY_CATCH}.</li>
    <li>You catch up to {M.PERSON_MAX} of mistakes, more the longer you look: {M.PERSON_MAX} × (1 − e<sup>−minutes/{M.PERSON_PACE}</sup>). In one large combined change, times {M.BIG_DIFF}; reading a plan before it is built, times {M.PLAN_READ}.</li>
    <li>Minutes: an agent review {M.AGENT_REVIEW_MIN}, planning {M.PLAN_MIN} per piece, the lead reading a report {M.LEAD_CHECK_MIN}, a subagent starting up {M.SPAWN_MIN}, you writing a brief {M.PROMPT_MIN}, you sorting out a conflict {M.MERGE_MIN}, comparing two results {M.COMPARE} reviews. Model class multiplies the agents’ times.</li>
    <li>Collisions: on fully tangled work with three or more other pieces in flight, {M.OVERLAP} of changes collide; half that when a plan or a lead divides the work. Of K pieces, about K<sup>{M.CHAIN_FLOOR}</sup> can run at once on tangled work and all K on independent work.</li>
    <li>Tokens, in units of one build: a redo {M.T_REDO}, an agent review {M.T_REVIEW}, planning {M.T_PLAN} per piece, the lead reading a report {M.T_CHECK}, and {M.T_START} for every fresh context. A thread pays that once; a subagent pays it every time it is started.</li>
    <li>For money on the cost tile: {M.UNIT_TOKENS.toLocaleString('en-GB')} tokens per unit, {M.OUTPUT_SHARE * 100}% of them output, at list prices with no caching.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Change any of these and the shapes of the results hold: more agents stop helping once you are the queue, tangled work leaves
    agents blocked, and checks by the same model miss what the builder missed. The exact hours do not hold.
  </p>

